---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Ticker from '../../components/Ticker.astro';
import Portrait from '../../components/Portrait.astro';
import Glyph from '../../components/Glyph.astro';
import Illustration from '../../components/Illustration.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import NextSteps from '../../components/NextSteps.astro';
import { faithIntegratedTherapyService, faqPage } from '../../data/schema';

const tickerItems = ['Meaning', 'Vocation', 'Discernment', 'Calling', 'Spiritual Dryness', 'Ministry', 'Faith & Doubt'];

const whoThisIsFor = [
  {
    title: 'The question is a real question',
    copy: 'You are facing something that does not resolve into a pros and cons list. A vocational decision. Whether to stay somewhere. What you are actually called to, and whether that word still means anything to you. You have prayed about it and you have thought about it and you keep arriving at the same place, which is nowhere.',
  },
  {
    title: 'It has gone quiet',
    copy: 'Your faith is intact. You have not lost it and you are not in crisis. It has simply gone flat, and it has been flat for a while. The practices that used to do something do not do it now. You are still showing up and you are not sure whether that is faithfulness or momentum. This has a long history in the contemplative tradition and it is often mistaken for depression, sometimes correctly and often not.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The life works and something is missing',
    copy: 'By the measures available, you are doing well. The career, the marriage, the house. And there is a persistent question underneath about whether any of it is what you were for. That question is usually treated as a midlife cliché. It is generally not a cliché. It is a real question that arrived late because you were busy.',
  },
  {
    title: 'You lead, and you have nowhere to say the true thing',
    copy: 'You are a pastor, on staff, in ministry, or in a role where people look to you for spiritual steadiness. Your work, your faith, your friendships, and your income are all in the same place. There is no room in that arrangement to say that you are not sure, or that you are tired in a way rest does not touch, or that you resent it some weeks.',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Are you a Christian therapist?',
    a: ['My training includes an M.A. in Spiritual Formation and Soul Care from Talbot School of Theology, so the Christian tradition is familiar territory and you will not need to translate it for me. I work with people across and outside that tradition. What matters clinically is that your spiritual life is real to you and belongs in the room, not that we hold the same positions.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'Will we pray together in session?',
    a: ['No. Some practices do this and it can be meaningful for the right person with the right clinician. It is not part of my work. Prayer inside a therapeutic relationship introduces an authority dynamic into a room where we are often examining exactly that, and I would rather keep the hour clear for a different kind of attention.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from spiritual direction?',
    a: ['Spiritual direction attends to your relationship with God and treats that relationship as the subject. Therapy attends to you: your history, your patterns, and the reasons you are the particular person having that relationship in the way you are. Many people find the two work well alongside each other, and I am glad to work with someone who already has a director.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'My faith has gone flat and I do not know if that is a spiritual problem or depression. Which is it?',
    a: ['Sometimes both, and the distinction is worth taking time over rather than settling quickly. Spiritual dryness has a long history in the contemplative tradition and is not in itself a clinical condition. Depression is, and it can also present as a loss of meaning that looks spiritual from the inside. Part of early work is getting a careful look at what is actually going on rather than assuming.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you work with pastors and people in vocational ministry?',
    a: ['Yes. When work, faith, community, and income all sit in the same place, there is often nowhere to say the honest thing. Sessions are confidential and are not shared with your church, your board, or your denomination.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'What if part of my spiritual history is painful rather than sustaining?',
    a: ['That is common and it belongs here too. Faith is rarely all one thing. If the harder material turns out to be the larger part of the picture, you may find the page on <a href="/services/religious-trauma-therapy/">religious trauma therapy</a> is closer to where you are, and either way we can work with both.'],
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you take insurance?',
    a: ['I am a private-pay practice. Many clients use out-of-network benefits through a PPO plan, and I provide a monthly superbill you can submit for reimbursement. I also accept HSA and FSA cards.'],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Faith-Integrated & Christian Therapy | Newport Beach, CA"
  description="Depth therapy that takes your spiritual life seriously. For meaning, vocation, discernment, and faith that has gone quiet. Newport Beach, Fullerton, and telehealth across California."
  active="/services/"
  path="/services/faith-integrated-therapy/"
  schema={[
    faithIntegratedTherapyService,
    faqPage(faqs, {
      id: 'https://nikolesparks.com/services/faith-integrated-therapy#faq',
      url: 'https://nikolesparks.com/services/faith-integrated-therapy',
    }),
  ]}
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/services/">Services</a> / Faith-integrated therapy</p>
      <h1>Your spiritual life is not <em>a separate appointment.</em></h1>
      <p>
        What would it feel like to have your faith understood as part of the whole picture? For
        many people, their faith is the ground from which they understand love, suffering,
        purpose, responsibility, forgiveness, and belonging. It informs the choices they make,
        the relationships they tend, and the life they hope to live. I offer depth-oriented,
        faith-integrated therapy in Newport Beach and Fullerton, and by telehealth throughout
        California.
      </p>
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Schedule a free consultation <span class="arw">→</span></a>
    </div>
    <!-- PLACEHOLDER hero illustration: swap for the custom SVG when provided. -->
    <Illustration name="grounding-full" size="clamp(380px, 44vw, 520px)" color="var(--blue)" class="hero-figure" />
  </section>

  <Ticker items={tickerItems} label="Where faith and life meet" />

  <!-- The premise -->
  <section class="section split c-wide-narrow">
    <div class="col gap-28">
      <p class="eyebrow">The premise</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif h2-lg">An invitation to bring <em>your faith</em> into the room.</h2>
      <p class="body">The questions that bring people to therapy often have a deeper shape beneath them. What does it mean to be faithful when the path forward is unclear? How do you make sense of relationships that have left you hurt, disappointed, or unsure of what love asks of you now? What do you do with regret, resentment, longing, or the ache of not knowing how to move forward from here?</p>
      <p class="body">These are not questions with generic answers. They are part of what it means to live a life before God, alongside other people, with all the complexity, limitation, and hope that entails.</p>
      <p class="body">Our work can offer a place to slow down and listen closely to what is happening beneath the surface. Together, we can make room for your experience in its fullness: the things that hurt, the patterns that have shaped you, the relationships that matter, and the faith through which you understand your life.</p>
    </div>
    <Illustration name="integration-full" size="clamp(300px, 32vw, 420px)" color="var(--blue)" class="hero-figure" />
  </section>

  <!-- Who this is for -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="stack gap-20" style="margin-bottom:48px">
      <p class="eyebrow">Who I work with</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif" style="max-width:760px">This is for people whose spiritual life is <em>load-bearing.</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="process-grid cols-2">
      {whoThisIsFor.map((c, i) => (
        <div class="process-card" style="border-top-color:var(--ink-30)">
          <span class="num" style="color:var(--blue)">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div class="title">{c.title}</div>
          <p style="color:var(--ink-85)">{c.copy}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What the work looks like -->
  <section class="section split c-narrow-wide">
    <div class="col gap-20">
      <p class="eyebrow">The work</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">Not spiritual advice. <em>Attention.</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="body">I am not going to tell you what God wants for you. I would not know, and a therapist who takes that position has quietly assumed an authority that does not belong in this room and will distort everything that follows.</p>
      <p class="body">What we do instead is pay close attention to what is actually happening in you, with your spiritual life included in the frame rather than set aside.</p>
      <p class="body">That means the material of the sessions is often ordinary. A resentment you noticed and dismissed. What happens in your body during a particular part of a service. The specific quality of the flatness, which is usually more textured than the word suggests once you slow down enough to describe it. Where your sense of obligation comes from, and whether it belongs to God or to somebody who spoke for God when you were nine.</p>
      <p class="body">Depth-oriented work assumes that the thing driving you is generally not the thing you would name if asked. So we go slowly, we follow what comes up, and we pay attention to what happens in the room when we get near certain material. Over time, things that arrived fused start to come apart. Being loved and being useful. Calling and obligation. Faithfulness and the inability to disappoint anyone.</p>
      <p class="body">What people often describe is not that they got an answer. It is that the question got clearer, and that they stopped needing to have already resolved it in order to live.</p>
      <p class="body">This is open-ended weekly work. <a href="/approach/" style="color:var(--blue); border-bottom:1px solid var(--blue)">My approach</a> has more on the thinking behind it, and <a href="/blog/what-happens-in-a-depth-therapy-session/" style="color:var(--blue); border-bottom:1px solid var(--blue)">what actually happens in a session</a> covers the mechanics.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Therapy and spiritual direction -->
  <section class="section blue on-color split c-wide-narrow">
    <div class="col gap-28">
      <p class="eyebrow on-color">Therapy &amp; spiritual direction</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif h2-lg">Related, and <em>not the same.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">If you already have a spiritual director, that relationship and this one can run alongside each other without conflict. They are asking different questions.</p>
      <p class="body">Spiritual direction attends to your relationship with God and treats that relationship as the subject. Therapy attends to you: your history, your patterns, the reasons you are the particular person having that relationship in the particular way you are having it. When a director notices that you cannot receive something, direction can name it. Therapy can go looking for where you learned not to receive things, which is usually somewhere with a name and an address.</p>
      <p class="body">Plenty of people find that the two together do something neither does alone. There is more on this in <a href="/blog/therapy-and-spiritual-direction/" style="color:var(--cream); border-bottom:1px solid var(--cream-35)">therapy and spiritual direction are not the same thing</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <Glyph name="nested-ellipses" class="hero-figure" />
  </section>

  <!-- Background -->
  <section class="section split c-media">
    <Portrait src="/nikole_sparks-mft-website.jpg" alt="Nikole Sparks" placeholder="Nikole — candid photo" />
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow">Background</p>
      <h2 class="serif h2-lg">Why I work <em class="accent">this way.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">I hold an M.A. in Spiritual Formation and Soul Care from Talbot School of Theology. That is not a credential I mention to establish agreement with you about anything in particular. It means the vocabulary is not foreign, the tradition is not exotic to me, and you will not spend sessions explaining your world before we can get to the thing you came in for.</p>
      <p class="body">I am also in postgraduate psychoanalytic training at the Newport Psychoanalytic Institute, which is where the depth orientation comes from.</p>
      <p class="body">Those two together are the reason I can take your faith seriously as real and still be curious about what formed it. Holding only the clinical side, a therapist tends to hear spiritual material and translate it into something else. Holding only the theological side, the instinct is to reassure. Neither leaves much room to actually look.</p>
      <p class="body">You do not need to share my beliefs for this to work, and I will not ask you what they are as a condition of anything.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/about/">About Nikole <span class="arw">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQs -->
  <section class="section" style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr; gap:80px; align-items:start">
    <div class="sticky-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Faith-integrated therapy</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <Illustration name="questions-full" size="240px" class="mt-16" />
    </div>
    <Faq items={faqs} />
  </section>

  <!-- The bridge to religious trauma therapy -->
  <section class="section sage on-color">
    <div class="col gap-20" style="max-width:760px">
      <p class="eyebrow on-color">A companion path</p>
      <h2 class="h2-serif">If what you are carrying is <em>heavier than this.</em></h2>
      <p class="body">Faith is rarely all one thing. Some of what you were given sustains you and some of it may have cost you something, and those can be true in the same person on the same Sunday.</p>
      <p class="body">If the harder part is the larger part right now, <a href="/services/religious-trauma-therapy/" style="color:var(--cream); border-bottom:1px solid var(--cream-35)">therapy for religious trauma</a> is where that work is described. It is the same practice and the same clinician. You do not need to have sorted out which page you belong on before you call, and most people do not arrive knowing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NextSteps
    heading="Bring the whole thing. <em>Let’s talk.</em>"
    copy="The free 15-minute consultation is a conversation, not an intake. In-person in Newport Beach and Fullerton, or telehealth throughout California."
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    section[style*='grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr'] {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
      gap: 40px !important;
    }
  }
</style>
